course: dictionary
lesson: 8223
title: Revenue: money coming in
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Revenue is a formal word for money that comes in. A shop, a business or a government gets revenue when it sells things or collects taxes. Revenue is counted before costs are taken away, so it is the total money in.
explain: Look at the clues in the example: «Cost is the money spent to make something; revenue, or takings, is the money from selling; profit is revenue minus cost.» Cost is money going out, revenue is money coming in, and profit is what is left over.
explain: In maths word problems, revenue is price times the number sold. If a stall sells 10 cakes at £2 each, its revenue is £20. Then take away the costs of making the cakes, and what remains is profit.
explain: Be careful with words that try to sound nicer. In politics, someone may call a new tax «revenue enhancement». The clue is that it is still a tax. When a word sounds too clever, ask: what money is actually moving, and in which direction?
fact: Revenue means the money a business or government gets in, before costs are taken away.
fact: Revenue is another word for income, but in business it usually means money from selling. A synonym called «takings» appears in the example sentence.
fact: Revenue = price × number sold. For example, 10 bikes at £50 each gives revenue of £500.
fact: Profit = revenue − costs. If a business has £800 revenue and £500 costs, its profit is £300.
fact: Governments get tax revenue from taxes, and they use it to pay for things like schools and roads.
exercise: «Cost is the money spent to make something; revenue, or takings, is the money from selling; profit is revenue minus cost.» What is revenue? | The money spent to make something | *The money a business gets from selling, before costs are taken away | Money a person saves in a bank | What is left after all costs are taken away
exercise: A shop sells 10 bikes at £50 each. What is its revenue? | *£500 | £60 | £40 | £5
exercise: In the sentence above, which word means takings? | *revenue | profit | cost | making
test: A government collects money from taxes. What is the right name for that money? | tax refund | tax loan | *tax revenue | tax holiday
test: A shop has revenue of £800 and costs of £500. What is its profit? | £1,300 | £500 | *£300 | £800
exam: Which formal word means the money a business gets in from selling, before costs are taken away? | debt | *revenue | penalty | expense
